<div class="lab-head">
  <h1>ICE CUBE &mdash; 6 CANDIDATES</h1>
  <p>
    Same post, same words, six visuals. Each card below is rendered through
    the real post shell, so the borders, spacing and mobile behaviour are the
    live ones. Pick one, promote it into src/posts/, delete this page and the
    five CSS files you did not keep.
    <a href="/index.html">&lt;&lt; back to home</a>
  </p>
</div>

{% set iceIntro %}
<p>
  <span class="post-opener"
    >An ice cube sits at -5&deg;C. You turn the heat on. At -4, its shape
    does not change. At -3, -2, -1, still the same cube, the same silhouette,
    the same silence.</span
  >
  But unchanged is not untouched. Each degree is energy already absorbed.
  Then the cube reaches 0&deg;C and the phase change begins.
</p>
{% endset %}

{% set iceOutro %}
<p>
  It is easy to look at that climb and call it wasted. Nothing melted. Nothing
  moved. Nothing worth showing anyone. Yet warming each gram of ice through
  those five degrees absorbed about 10.5 joules. The threshold did not create
  that energy. It revealed where it had been going.
</p>
<p>
  Here is the better part of the story. Melting that same gram takes about 334
  more joules, roughly 32 times the entire climb from -5&deg;C to 0&deg;C. In a
  simple lab model, the temperature stays near 0&deg;C while the phase change
  continues. The reading can go flat precisely when the deepest transformation
  begins.
</p>
<p>
  Progress can look like that. The reps, drafts, applications, and awkward
  first attempts may leave no result anyone can point to. But the metaphor has
  a limit: effort is not heat. Bad reps do not become mastery just because you
  repeat them.
</p>
<hr class="post-divider" />
<p>
  So check the direction. Listen to the feedback. Fix the process. Then, if
  the work is sound, do not mistake a flat reading for wasted effort. The
  breakthrough was never one magic degree. It was every degree before it, plus
  the long transformation after. Keep the heat on.
</p>
<p>
  <small>
    Inspired by James Clear&rsquo;s
    <a
      href="https://jamesclear.com/quotes/complaining-about-not-achieving-success-despite-working-hard-is-like-complaining-about-an-ice-cube-not-melting-when-you-heated-it-from-twenty-five-to-thirty-one-degrees-your-work-was-not-was"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >ice-cube analogy</a
    >. The simplified model assumes reasonably pure ice near ordinary
    pressure. Thermal values are rounded from
    <a
      href="https://www.chem.tamu.edu/class/fyp/chem101-portfolio/classroomwork.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >Texas A&amp;M chemistry tables</a
    >.
  </small>
</p>
{% endset %}

